WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.620
In theoretical physics, especially in the
theory of relativity and quantum mechanics,

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:09.800
we will regularly encounter symmetric and
antisymmetric tensors.

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:15.460
A symmetric tensor t_{ij} remains the same
if we swap its indices.

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:20.430
Specifically, swapping the indices of the
second-level tensor as a matrix means that

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:25.640
the matrix remains the same if we transpose
it, in other words if we mirror the rows and

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:27.590
columns on the diagonal.

00:00:27.590 --> 00:00:33.820
This symmetry property of tensors is very
useful and simplifies calculations in computer

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:35.460
physics enormously.

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:40.710
Moreover, this property is crucial in quantum
mechanics, because symmetric matrices have

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:42.550
real eigenvalues.

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:47.020
They therefore represent physical quantities
(we call them observables) that we can measure

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:48.960
in an experiment.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:53.500
So if you have a symmetric tensor in front
of you, as a theoretical physicist you should

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:55.750
immediately get a dopamine kick.

00:00:55.750 --> 00:01:00.660
The Kronecker delta, for example, is an example
of a simple symmetric tensor.

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:03.210
We have considered a second-order tensor.

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:05.780
What if the tensor is of a HIGHER order?

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:08.410
What about its symmetry property THEN?

00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:13.320
For example, if the tensor has four indices
and it remains EQUAL when we swap the first

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:18.420
two indices, then we are talking about a symmetric
tensor in the indices m and k.

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:23.390
However, we will also encounter tensors that
are antisymmetric.

00:01:23.390 --> 00:01:28.850
An antisymmetric tensor changes sign when
we swap its indices.

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:34.060
If the antisymmetric tensor is represented
as a matrix, then it is equal to its negative

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:35.500
transpose.

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:40.340
Unfortunately, most tensors are neither symmetric
nor antisymmetric.

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:46.180
But the great thing is: Mathematically, we
can split every tensor T into a symmetric

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:51.869
and an antisymmetric part: T is equal to S
plus A.

00:01:51.869 --> 00:01:56.909
Let's take a look at how we practically split
a general second-level tensor T into its two

00:01:56.909 --> 00:01:58.200
parts.

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:05.120
The symmetric part S of the tensor T is "One
and a half times t_{ij} plus t_{ji}.

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:08.140
In the second term, we have swapped the two
indices.

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:10.479
Then we added the two tensors together.

00:02:10.479 --> 00:02:15.900
The factor of one and a half is important
because we have counted the tensor twice here.

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:23.200
The antisymmetric part A of the tensor T is
"One and a half times t_{ij}, minus t_{ji}.

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:28.030
Here we have swapped the two indices, which
gives the swapped tensor a minus sign.

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:31.670
The factor of one and a half must not be missing
here either.

00:02:31.670 --> 00:02:36.160
We then add the symmetric and antisymmetric
parts together to obtain the total tensor.

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:37.160
We are done.

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:41.650
The first summand is the symmetric part of
the tensor t_{ij} and the second summand is

00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:43.170
the antisymmetric part.

